          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why do some categories need a fourth or fifth character while others do not?",
          "answer": "Some categories are complete at three characters, and others need more detail to be a full, billable code.\n\nThe ICD-10-CM structure document says characters four and five add detail such as cause, anatomical site, or severity. Character six often records laterality (right, left, or unspecified). A seventh character, where a category uses one, describes the encounter rather than the disease, such as initial treatment or follow-up for an injury. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\nThe hypertension document gives I10 as an example of a complete three-character code. It says most categories require a fourth, fifth, or sixth character, and that I10 takes none. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n\nThe COPD document shows what an extra character can do. In J44, the fourth character records whether a complication, such as an acute lower respiratory infection or an acute exacerbation, is present at the encounter. (Source: *Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes*.)\n\nThe type 2 diabetes document shows how the chapter separates diabetes by cause, and that E11 has its own three-character category. It also notes that an unqualified diagnosis of diabetes defaults to type 2. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n\nThe retrieved passages describe which characters do what, but they do not explain the reasoning behind why some categories were split further than others. For that, you would need a coding reference or a certified coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
